{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfiHLdxR1tY25yb8dZues","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written the candidate for slot 3 and it passes the self-check. I haven't confirmed it will survive admission's full fit, which may only just finish inside the 30-minute limit.\n\n**What I refined:** the incumbent, `length_normalised_chance_vs_motif`. I named the new model `person_specific_chance_switch_belief`.\n\n**The one change:** in the incumbent everyone shares a single belief about how often a fair coin switches sides. In the new model each person has their own belief, drawn from a population distribution with 400 slots so new participants can be predicted. That means some people can think chance is streaky and pick the sequence with fewer switches. This targets the critique's one finding that survives the FDR correction: 18.8% of participants prefer the fewer-switch sequence, against 5% predicted (q = 0.016). Everything else is as in the incumbent: the over-alternating fair coin compared against the Markov, biased and repeating-motif generators, the length normalisation, and the person-specific sensitivity.\n\n**One deviation from the literal hypothesis:** my first version multiplied each person's sensitivity by their switch belief. To avoid that awkward shape for the sampler, the model fits the product as one per-person weight. The predictions are the same, and a negative weight means a person who believes chance is streaky.\n\n**Self-check results:**\n- **Result:** it printed `OK`.\n- **Smoke-fit ELPD-LOO:** −2111.8. This is a 1-chain, 100-draw fit, so it is not directly comparable with the incumbent's −2318.3 from the full fit.\n- **PSIS-LOO reliability:** about 6.4% of trials have Pareto k above 0.5, past the 1% tolerance. The per-person parameters are the likely cause. Admission only needs a finite ELPD, but if this persists in the full fit the model's estimate would be flagged unreliable and it could not be exported as the winner.\n- **Convergence:** not checked, because a single chain cannot give an R-hat.\n\n**Speed:** in a 600-iteration timing run on one chain it sampled at about the incumbent's speed (128 s vs 122 s, same tree depth). Scaled up to the full 7,000 iterations that is roughly 24 minutes per chain, close to the 30-minute admission limit. The incumbent has the same cost and was admitted.\n\nFiles are in `/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment2/model_loop/iter_0/candidate_3/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":962,"cache_read_input_tokens":73063,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":962,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8a39b781-ca0b-498d-b825-844579ae6e96","uuid":"06644b08-c456-4d4c-9fde-3cd33b0fb937","timestamp":"2026-10-05T02:14:14.081Z","request_id":"req_011CfiHLcx8uDMXFfFnNwvqQ"}
